.TH SAFESHELL 1 "November 2026" "safeshell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
safeshell \- a safety layer for your terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B safeshell
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B safeshell
rates every shell command by how reversible it is (R0 read-only to R4
catastrophic), can run it in a rootless sandbox first to show what it would
change, blocks the dangerous ones with a reason and a safer alternative, and
keeps a snapshot so the last approved command can be undone.
.PP
Run
.B safeshell install
once to add the hook to your shell startup file. After that every command you
press Enter on in bash, zsh or fish is checked first.
.SH COMMANDS
Every command accepts
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-help ,
which prints its help and does nothing else.
.TP
.BI "check [--json] " command
Rate a command without running it. Exit status 0 means R0 to R2, 3 means R3, 4 means R4.
.TP
.BI "check --script " FILE... " [--format text|json|sarif|github] [--fail-on R2|R3|R4]"
Rate every command in one or more shell scripts without running them; only the built-in rules are used, never the sandbox, the AI or the daemon. Each command that is not R0 is listed with the line it starts on, the level and the reason; the result is the highest level over all files. A script that is not valid shell or cannot be read is reported and the other files are still checked (exit status 2 if nothing worse). \fB\-\-format\fR chooses text (default), json (one object per file with path, level and lines), sarif (SARIF 2.1.0) or github (workflow annotations, ::error for R3 and R4 and ::warning for R2). The exit status is that of the highest level when it is at least \fB\-\-fail-on\fR (default R3: 3 for R3, 4 for R4; 1 for R2), otherwise 0.
.TP
.BI "analyze [--json] [--verbose] [--cwd " DIR "] " command
Rate a command, run it in the sandbox, and report the real files, processes and disks it would touch.
.TP
.BI "analyze [--verbose] [--cwd " DIR "] --script " FILE
The line-by-line rating of a script, then the whole script run in the sandbox. The result is the highest of the line levels and what the run showed. Without a sandbox the run is estimated only. Exit status as for a command.
.TP
.BI "simulate [--json] [--verbose] [--cwd " DIR "] [--timeout " DURATION "] " command
Run a command in the sandbox and show what it changed. Real files are never touched.
.TP
.BI "explain [--why] " command
Analysis plus a plain-English explanation. Needs an AI model.
With
.B \-\-why
it instead shows which rule and which evidence (ESTIMATED, INSPECTED, PREVIEWED or OBSERVED)
set the final level; this works without an AI, and the explanation is added when one is set.
.TP
.BI ask " request"
Turn a request written in English into a command, which is then checked like any other.
The assistant is told the project in this folder (git branch, Makefile targets, npm scripts), the
commands SafeShell saw in the last 30 minutes and, in a terminal with the hook, the last few
requests, so a follow-up such as "now delete them" or "undo that" is understood.
See
.B safeshell help privacy
for what a cloud model receives.
.TP
.RB "models [" list ]
Choose the AI model from a menu, or list the models your cloud key offers.
.TP
.B ai setup|status|stop|bench
Manage the optional built-in AI. Nothing is downloaded until you run
.BR "ai setup" .
.TP
.B voice [setup [\-\-yes] [\-\-model base.en|base|small] [\-\-language CODE]|status|\-\-text]
Speak a request. The speech is turned into text on this machine and then handled like
typed English. Run
.B voice setup
once to install the tools it needs (cmake, g++ and a recorder, with apt-get after asking;
.B \-\-yes
skips the question), build the speech engine and download the model.
.B \-\-model
chooses base.en (English only, the default), base or small (many languages);
.B \-\-language
is a two or three letter code such as hi or es, or auto. The choice is saved in the config file and
.B voice status
shows it. The earlier model file is kept until the new one is downloaded and verified.
.TP
.RB "undo [--yes] [" ID ]
Put back what an approved command changed, and verify it. With no ID it offers the newest snapshot from this terminal, else this folder, else any, shows the command, time, folder and file count, and asks before restoring. Files changed since the command ran are listed and need a full "yes". A snapshot already undone is skipped unless its ID is given. Undo restores contents, permissions and times, recreates pipes, and writes a hard-linked file back in place; it then checks every path and lists anything that differs: a socket or device it cannot recreate, other hard links it could not restore, new files added to a saved folder (left alone). Paths that could not be read are left out of the snapshot and named. With no terminal to ask, nothing is restored and the command to confirm is printed.
.B \-\-yes
answers the question.
.TP
.B snapshots
List saved snapshots and their total size.
.TP
.BR "snapshots prune" " [--older " AGE "] [--max " SIZE ]
Delete the oldest snapshots until none is older than
.I AGE
(such as 30d or 12h) and all together fit in
.I SIZE
(such as 5G or 500M). Without options the limits come from
.I snapshots.keep_days
and
.I snapshots.max_gib
in the config file (30 days and 5 GiB by default); the same limits are applied automatically after each new snapshot. A snapshot is refused before anything is copied when the files are over 2 GiB or the disk holding snapshots has too little free space.
.TP
.BI "history [-n " LINES "] [--json]"
Print decisions SafeShell recorded, newest first.
.TP
.B daemon [stop]
Keep SafeShell warm in the background for faster checks. A daemon left running from an older version exits on its first request and checks run directly until it is restarted. stop waits for the daemon to exit.
.TP
.B deactivate, activate
Pause checking in every terminal, open and new, and turn it back on. Nothing needs restarting; while paused the hook costs nothing. SAFESHELL_OFF=1 pauses one terminal only.
.TP
.B learn [on|off|status]
Learning mode, off by default. When on, each command that passes without a warning gets one line saying what it does, its level and the safer form. It only adds output.
.TP
.B install [--ai], uninstall
Add or remove the shell hook. Running install again after moving or reinstalling safeshell updates the hook to the new path.
.TP
.B hook bash|zsh|fish
Print the shell hook.
.TP
.B doctor
Check every part and say what to fix: sandbox, hook (installed and active in this terminal), daemon, snapshots, AI and cloud key. It only reads.
.TP
.B tour
A one-minute walk-through in a throwaway folder: an R0 command, a blocked rm -rf, a snapshot and an undo. Nothing is left behind.
The hook mentions the tour once per user, in a terminal.
.TP
.BI "guard [--cwd " DIR "] \-\- " command
What the shell hook runs for each command line: analyse it, ask if needed, and print the command to run.
Exit status 0 means run the printed command, 1 means it was cancelled, 2 means wrong usage.
.TP
.B "guard \-\-agent"
For AI coding agents: read one JSON command on stdin and answer allow or deny on stdout, with the reason and a safer option, never asking a question.
It accepts a Claude Code PreToolUse hook input or {"command": "...", "cwd": "..."}.
Nobody is there to confirm, so anything that is not a plain allow is denied, and so is input it cannot read or a check that takes too long.
Each answer is logged as agent-allowed or agent-denied.
Exit status 0 means allowed (a Claude Code hook is always answered with 0), 2 means denied.
.TP
.BR "agent snippet " "claude" | wrapper
Print the hook to add to Claude Code's settings.json, or a script that runs one command only when SafeShell allows it. Nothing is edited.
.TP
.RB "help [" topic ]
Short guides: start, safety, english, models, voice, learn, pause, agents, undo, policy, hosts, commands, privacy and trouble.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/safeshell/policy.json
Optional team policy written by an administrator: rules that block a command with a reason, raise its level or ask for a snapshot. It can only make SafeShell stricter. It is used only when root owns it and its folder and nobody else can write to them; otherwise it is ignored with a warning. safeshell doctor shows which policy is active.
.TP
.I ~/.config/safeshell/config.json
Settings. The "hosts" key maps host patterns (* matches any text) to the label prod or staging; a command that changes something on a prod host over ssh is raised one level, and an interactive ssh to a labelled host prints a note that commands there are only checked if SafeShell is installed on it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/safeshell/keys.json
API keys for cloud models, readable only by you.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/safeshell/audit.jsonl
Log of decisions.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/safeshell/off
Exists while checking is paused (safeshell deactivate).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/safeshell/snapshots/
Snapshots that safeshell undo restores from.
.PP
The folders follow
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME " and " XDG_DATA_HOME
when they are set.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SAFESHELL_OFF
When set to anything, even 0, the hook in that terminal does not check commands. Unset it to check again.
.TP
.B SAFESHELL_NODAEMON
When set, checks never use the background daemon and run directly.
.TP
.B SAFESHELL_AI
Override the AI mode: auto, off, local, cloud or hybrid.
.TP
.BR ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ", " OPENAI_API_KEY ", " GEMINI_API_KEY
Cloud model keys, used instead of the key file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.BR check ", " analyze
0 for R0 to R2, 3 for R3, 4 for R4.
.TP
.B guard
0 run the printed command, 1 cancelled (also after Ctrl-C), 2 wrong usage.
.TP
.B guard \-\-agent
0 allowed, 2 denied.
.TP
.BR analyze ", " simulate " after Ctrl-C"
130; nothing is run and the sandbox's scratch folder is removed.
.TP
.B other commands
0 on success, 1 on failure, 2 for wrong usage.
.SH NOTES
The program is called
.B safeshell
and not
.B ss
because
.BR ss (8),
the socket statistics tool, already uses that name.
